  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nThis dry and warm pattern will continue for a few more days before \ntrue attempts to break the ridge down are made. Today, a shortwave \nwill travel east across the area dampening the ridge a little before \nit builds back almost immediately. This shortwave should pass \nuneventful with no rain. Any rain activity should remain off to the \nnorth and west.\n\nThe next shortwave will cause a weak backdoor cold front to dip down \ninto central IL on Thursday, then retreat back north as a warm front \nFriday, possibly bringing some very light rain north of I-72. \nHowever, it could also pass without any activity due to how dry our \nairmass is. As of now, there is a 10-30% chance of showers for \ndaytime Thursday and a 20-40% chance of showers again Friday.\n\nAnother disturbance will follow closely behind Saturday, bringing a \n30-50% chance for showers Saturday into Sunday. Amounts are forecast \nto be minimal (< 0.5 inch area wide). Whatever light rain manages to \nfall will do little to alleviate the ongoing dry stretch.\n\nA much stronger low pressure system is shown to move through early \nnext week. The Euro model is showing a deeper, stronger closed upper \nlow (compared to the GFS and Canadian) traversing over the southern \nPlains. The Euro has the low center having a much more north track \nthan the other, with the center moving into Iowa. The GFS has the \nupper low center moving across the Ohio Valley. The Euro depiction \nwill affect central IL more than the GFS forecast. This system will \nbring the greatest chances for showers and thunderstorms to the area \nwithin the next week. There is a widespread 40-60% chance across \ncentral and southeastern IL. \n\nTemperatures will continue to be above normal. Highs will be in the \nmid to upper 70s, nearing 80 in some places through Friday. Lows \nwill be in the 50s. Temperatures will trend slightly cooler starting \nSaturday into next week. \"Cooler\" in this sense means temperatures \nwill be in the low 70s for highs, still above normal. The CPC's 6-10 \nday, 8-14 day, and Week 3-4 outlooks are all still showing above \nnormal temperatures.",
